# Netlify redirects.
#
# The canonical form of every page is the trailing slash, because the pages are
# directories on disk — `/mappa/index.html`, not `/mappa.html`. That form is
# host-agnostic: it stays correct if the site ever moves off Netlify, whereas
# relying on Netlify's own `.html` stripping does not.
#
# Netlify normally adds the slash itself, but making it explicit means the bare
# form redirects the same way whatever the site settings say — and one URL per
# page is the whole point of having a canonical.
#
# **Not forced (`301`, not `301!`), and that is the whole difference between a
# site and an outage.** Netlify matches a rule's path with and without its
# trailing slash, so `/mappa` also matches `/mappa/` — and `!` means "apply even
# when the file exists". Together that sent `/mappa/` to `/mappa/`, for ever:
# measured on the first deploy, fifty redirects and the client gives up. Every
# page but the home was unreachable. Without the force flag the existing file
# wins, so `/mappa/` is served and only the bare `/mappa` is redirected — which
# is what these lines were for.

/mappa           /mappa/           301
/come-funziona   /come-funziona/   301
/fonte-dati      /fonte-dati/      301
/privacy         /privacy/         301

/en              /en/              301
/en/map          /en/map/          301
/en/how-it-works /en/how-it-works/ 301
/en/data-source  /en/data-source/  301
/en/privacy      /en/privacy/      301

# The app used to be the whole site. Anything that bookmarked or linked the old
# single-page entry points should land on the map, not on the pitch.
#
# All five `index.html` forms are listed, not just these two. Netlify's Pretty
# URLs setting would strip them anyway — but that is the setting this file exists
# to not depend on, and covering two pages out of five made the guarantee true
# for `/` and `/mappa/` and a hope for the other three. Every one of these is a
# single hop straight to the canonical form: no chain, and each target is exactly
# the URL its page's `<link rel="canonical">` names.
/index.html                /                 301!
/mappa/index.html          /mappa/           301!
/come-funziona/index.html  /come-funziona/   301!
/fonte-dati/index.html     /fonte-dati/      301!
/privacy/index.html        /privacy/         301!

/en/index.html               /en/                301!
/en/map/index.html           /en/map/            301!
/en/how-it-works/index.html  /en/how-it-works/   301!
/en/data-source/index.html   /en/data-source/    301!
/en/privacy/index.html       /en/privacy/        301!

# Deliberately no language redirect on `/`. The Italian page stays the page at
# the root for everyone, including the crawler that sends no Accept-Language and
# the reader who was handed the link: a root that serves two documents depending
# on a header makes `hreflang` a statement about something nobody can check, and
# turns one URL into two things to cache. `x-default` points here, and the switch
# in the header is how a reader chooses.

# The Netlify subdomain is a second, fully crawlable copy of this site.
#
# `dovebus.netlify.app` answers 200 on every path, serves its own `robots.txt`
# saying `Allow: /`, and was measured doing exactly that. The pages do carry a
# canonical pointing at the apex, which is why this is a duplicate rather than an
# outage — but a canonical is a hint a search engine may decline, and on a domain
# with no authority yet there is no reason to put it to the vote.
#
# Host-qualified, so it matches *only* that hostname: the apex serves these paths
# untouched, and `deploy-preview-N--dovebus.netlify.app` and branch deploys have
# different hostnames and keep working as previews.
#
# `301!` is forced here for the reason the block above warns about — without the
# force flag the existing file wins and the rule never fires — and it is safe for
# the same reason it was dangerous there: the source names a host that is not the
# one being served, so it cannot match a request for a page on the real site.
#
# The target is the production origin. It is spelled out because a `_redirects`
# file is static, and `SITE_URL` is a build input — so `vite.config.ts` refuses
# to build when the two disagree, rather than letting this line rot.
https://dovebus.netlify.app/*  https://dovebus.it/:splat  301!
